QB.push(
{id:1301,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Why does the per-message cost of a long-running chat built on the Messages API rise as the conversation grows, even when the user's typed message stays the same length?",
o:["A. The API charges a session-maintenance fee for each turn kept alive","B. Output tokens are re-billed each time an earlier assistant reply is displayed","C. The API is stateless, so the client resends the whole accumulated history as input tokens on every request","D. Context-window pressure forces a larger `max_tokens` on each successive request"],a:2,
e:"The Messages API is **stateless**: it stores nothing between calls, so continuity exists only because your client resends the entire accumulated history. Turn 40 therefore pays for turns 1-39 again as input tokens. Two mitigations follow directly from the mechanism: put the stable prefix behind `cache_control` so re-sent tokens read at 0.1x base input instead of full price, and bound history growth by trimming or summarising old turns. The transferable principle: in a stateless protocol, conversation length is a cost variable your application owns, not the provider's.",
w:"A — there is no session or connection charge; billing is per request on tokens. B — output tokens are billed once, when generated; re-sent assistant turns are billed as input. D — `max_tokens` is an output cap you choose; it does not grow on its own and it is not what makes long chats expensive."},

{id:1302,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A multi-tenant assistant sends a 400-token tenant-specific preamble followed by a 9,000-token shared policy corpus, with one cache breakpoint at the end of the corpus. Across all tenants `cache_read_input_tokens` is near zero and `cache_creation_input_tokens` is high. What is the correct fix?",
o:["A. Put the shared corpus first and the tenant preamble after it, with the breakpoint at the end of the shared block","B. Allocate one cache breakpoint per tenant so each tenant gets a private cached prefix","C. Switch to the 1-hour cache TTL so each tenant's prefix survives between sessions","D. Move the tenant preamble into the top-level `system` field and the shared corpus into the first `user` message"],a:0,
e:"A cache entry is matched on a **prefix from the very start of the request**, in the render order `tools` -> `system` -> `messages`. Anything tenant-specific placed ahead of the shared text makes every tenant's prefix a different byte sequence, so no tenant can ever read another's entry and each pays a write at 1.25x base input. Reordering so the shared 9,000 tokens come first lets all tenants share one cached prefix, with the volatile tenant text after the breakpoint. Principle: order request content by **stability, most stable first**.",
w:"B — the limit is 4 breakpoints per request (a 5th returns 400), and per-tenant prefixes forfeit the shared reuse that is the whole point here. C — a longer TTL makes writes cost 2x base input; it does not make a mismatched prefix match. D — `system` is rendered before `messages`, so tenant text there still precedes the shared block and still breaks the shared prefix."},

{id:1303,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A multi-tenant assistant stores conversations keyed by a `tenant_id` that the browser sends in the request body. A support engineer discovers that changing that value in the browser's developer tools returns another tenant's conversation. What is the correct remedy?",
o:["A. Add a system-prompt instruction telling Claude never to reveal another tenant's data","B. Encrypt each conversation with a per-tenant key while keeping the client-supplied `tenant_id` as the lookup key","C. Issue each tenant a separate API key so usage and data are attributed correctly","D. Resolve the tenant from the authenticated server-side session and scope every store read and write to it, ignoring any tenant identifier supplied by the client"],a:3,
e:"Authorisation must be evaluated against the **authenticated session**, never against an identity supplied by the caller or asserted by the model. The tenant key is a server-derived value; the client's copy is at best a hint and at worst an attack. The same rule governs tool arguments in agent designs: treat model-supplied identifiers as untrusted input and re-authorise them in code. The transferable principle: **the model, and the client, are not security boundaries** - deterministic server-side code is.",
w:"A — prompt instructions are advisory; the leak happens in your data layer before the model is involved. B — encryption at rest does nothing when the attacker supplies the lookup key and your code decrypts on their behalf. C — per-tenant keys change billing attribution, not the authorisation defect in the conversation store."},

{id:1304,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A support tool sends a 780,000-token product manual on every request to a 1M-context model. p95 latency is 90 seconds, cost per question is high, and answers frequently miss details that are genuinely present in the middle of the document. What is the sound redesign?",
o:["A. Raise `max_tokens` so the model has room to quote more of the manual","B. Retrieve only the passages relevant to each question and send those, keeping the stable instructions and tools in a cached prefix","C. Split the manual across four cache breakpoints so all of it stays resident in cache","D. Move the manual into the top-level `system` field, where it is not counted as input"],a:1,
e:"A large context window is a **capability, not a budget to fill**. Every token in the request is read on every call, so stuffing 780K tokens buys latency and cost proportional to the whole corpus while attention is spread thin across mostly irrelevant text - which is why mid-document detail is missed. Retrieval inverts the ratio: a few thousand relevant tokens per question, plus a small cached instruction prefix. Principle: **select context per request; do not pre-load everything you might need.**",
w:"A — `max_tokens` caps generated output; it has no bearing on how much input is read or on retrieval quality. C — cache reads cost 0.1x base input but are still read and still add latency; caching makes a bad layout cheaper, not correct. D — `system` content is billed as input exactly like messages, and it is rendered before them in the cacheable prefix."},

{id:1305,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An internal HR assistant is grounded on a policy corpus that totals about 14,000 tokens and changes once a quarter. The team has built a chunking pipeline and a vector store, and is now debugging cases where the right paragraph is not retrieved. What is the better architecture?",
o:["A. Keep the vector store but raise top-k to 20 so the right chunk is always in the window","B. Send the entire 14,000-token corpus as a cached prefix on every request and remove the retrieval layer","C. Summarise the corpus down to 2,000 tokens and send the summary","D. Split the corpus across several requests and merge the answers in application code"],a:1,
e:"Retrieval exists to solve a problem this corpus does not have: it does not fit. 14,000 tokens fit comfortably, so sending all of it behind a `cache_control` breakpoint gives the model complete context with **zero recall risk**, at 0.1x base input per read once warm, and the quarterly change is a cache write you pay four times a year. The retrieval pipeline adds chunking, embedding, an index to operate, and a new failure mode - missed retrieval - in exchange for nothing. Principle: **choose retrieval when the corpus exceeds what you can afford to send, not by default.**",
w:"A — a larger top-k raises cost and dilutes context while leaving the recall failure mode in place. C — summarising discards the exact policy wording the assistant is supposed to quote. D — splitting a 14,000-token corpus across requests fragments context and forces the application to merge answers it cannot reliably reconcile."},

{id:1306,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"An interactive report feature makes users wait about 25 seconds while roughly 8,000 tokens are generated. A developer proposes moving it to the Message Batches API to halve the cost. What is the objection?",
o:["A. Batch requests cannot exceed 4,096 output tokens","B. Prompt caching is unavailable inside a batch","C. Batch results are billed at the standard rate if they complete in under an hour","D. A batch is only guaranteed to complete within 24 hours, which is incompatible with a user waiting at a screen"],a:3,
e:"The Batch API's roughly **50% discount is paid for with latency tolerance**: submissions complete **within 24 hours**, with no guarantee of sooner. That makes it right for overnight re-classification and wrong for anything a human is waiting on. For this feature the correct lever is streaming - set `\"stream\": true` so text appears within a second or two and the 25-second total becomes a progressive read rather than a blank spinner. Principle: **match the interaction model to who is waiting.**",
w:"A — batches carry no special output-token ceiling; the model's limits apply as usual. B — caching works with batches, and the 1-hour TTL is specifically recommended there because the 5-minute default can expire mid-batch. C — the discount is a property of the endpoint, not of how quickly a particular batch happens to finish."},

{id:1307,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A backend requests `\"stream\": true` and forwards the events to the browser. Server logs show `content_block_delta` events arriving within 700 ms, but the browser shows nothing for 22 seconds and then the entire answer at once. What is the most likely cause?",
o:["A. A hop between the backend and the browser is buffering the response body; streaming only works if every intermediary forwards chunks as they arrive","B. The browser must itself send `\"stream\": true` to the backend before the backend's stream is honoured","C. Text cannot be displayed before `message_delta`, which arrives only at the end","D. The backend should stop streaming and instead paginate the completed text to the client"],a:0,
e:"Streaming is an **end-to-end property**. A reverse proxy, load balancer, API gateway, or compression layer that buffers the response body will hold every chunk until the upstream connection closes, producing exactly this symptom: fast deltas server-side, one late blob client-side. The fix is configuration at the buffering hop - disable response buffering for the streaming route and forward with chunked transfer or SSE passthrough. Principle: **an architectural property only holds if every component in the path preserves it.**",
w:"B — `\"stream\": true` governs the call to the Messages API; how your own backend talks to its clients is your protocol choice. C — text arrives in `content_block_delta` throughout the response; `message_delta` carries the final `stop_reason` and cumulative output usage, not the text. D — abandoning streaming accepts the symptom as the design instead of fixing the one hop that breaks it."},

{id:1308,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A moderation gate classifies user posts with Claude before they become publicly visible. During a wave of `529 overloaded_error` responses the gate starts failing. Which degraded behaviour is appropriate?",
o:["A. Publish immediately and re-scan asynchronously, reversing any bad decision once the API recovers","B. Retry indefinitely until the API responds, because the check is mandatory","C. Hold affected posts in a review queue and show the author a clear pending state, because a control that exists to prevent harm must fail closed","D. Fall back to a keyword blocklist and treat its verdict as equivalent to the model's"],a:2,
e:"Degraded modes must be chosen by **what the component protects**. A moderation gate exists to prevent harmful content from becoming visible, so unavailability of the classifier must not become availability of unclassified content: it fails **closed**, into a queue with an honest user-visible state. Compare a product-recommendation panel, where failing open to a static list is entirely correct. Principle: **decide fail-open versus fail-closed from the cost of the error, not from the desire to stay available.**",
w:"A — publishing first makes the harm the control was built to prevent, and retraction does not undo distribution. B — unbounded retries during a 529 wave amplify the outage and still leave the posts in limbo, with no defined user-facing state. D — a blocklist is a reasonable stopgap signal, but declaring it equivalent silently lowers the safety bar without anyone deciding to."},

{id:1309,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A cost-reduction design routes every request to a cheap model first and escalates to an expensive model whenever the cheap model's self-reported confidence is low. Measured escalation rate is 34%. The monthly bill has barely moved. What is the economic explanation?",
o:["A. Escalated requests pay for both tiers, and each model has its own cache namespace, so the saving is entirely determined by the escalation rate and the lost cache reuse","B. The cheap model's output tokens are re-billed at the expensive model's rate once escalation occurs","C. Cascades are incompatible with prompt caching, so all cached savings are forfeited","D. The routing decision itself is billed as output tokens, which dominates the cost"],a:0,
e:"A cascade only saves money on requests that **terminate at the cheap tier**. At a 34% escalation rate, a third of traffic pays the cheap call plus the expensive call, and the cheap calls on that third are pure waste. Worse, **prompt caches are model-scoped**: splitting traffic across two models splits the cache, so each model's prefix warms more slowly and more requests pay a write at 1.25x base input instead of a read at 0.1x. Judge the design on **cost per completed task**, and compare it against the simpler alternative of one capable model at a lower `output_config.effort`.",
w:"B — each model is billed at its own rates for the tokens it actually processed; there is no retroactive repricing. C — caching works fine in a cascade; the problem is that the cache is per model, so reuse is diluted rather than eliminated. D — a routing call is dominated by input tokens and is small; it is not what neutralises the saving."},

{id:1310,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"To remove a network hop, a team proposes that their backend mint a 15-minute API credential and hand it to the single-page app so the browser can call the Messages API directly, with CORS restricted to their origin. Why is this still the wrong architecture?",
o:["A. It is acceptable, provided the credential lifetime stays under 15 minutes and it is delivered over TLS","B. It is acceptable, because a restricted CORS origin prevents other sites from using the credential","C. Any credential the browser holds grants full API access for its lifetime and cannot be scoped to one user or one budget, so the call must stay behind a backend that authorises, meters, validates and logs it","D. It is acceptable if the client also sends `metadata.user_id` so per-user usage can be attributed"],a:2,
e:"An API credential is an **organisation-level capability**, not a user-level one: whoever holds it can send any request, with any prompt, at any volume, until it expires. Nothing in the credential expresses \"this user, this feature, this spend\". Only a **backend-mediated** design can bind a call to an authenticated user, enforce per-user quotas, validate and sanitise input, redact and log for audit, and rotate the credential centrally. Principle: **the API key belongs to a trust domain the client is not in.**",
w:"A — a short lifetime shrinks the exposure window; it does not create per-user scoping or spend control, and 15 minutes is ample for abuse. B — CORS is enforced by cooperating browsers only; a credential extracted from devtools is replayed with `curl`, where CORS is irrelevant. D — `metadata` is an opaque attribution field for your own reporting; it carries no authorisation weight whatsoever."},

{id:1311,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A retail assistant caches a prefix containing its instructions, its tool definitions, and a live stock table that is refreshed every 10 minutes. The team selected the 1-hour cache TTL to \"maximise reuse\". `usage` shows large `cache_creation_input_tokens` on most requests and small `cache_read_input_tokens`, and spend has roughly doubled. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Lower the TTL to 5 minutes so each rewrite is cheaper","B. Keep the stock table out of the cached prefix and pass it per request after the final breakpoint, caching only the stable instructions and tool definitions","C. Add a second breakpoint immediately after the stock table so only that section is rewritten","D. Serialise the stock table deterministically so the prefix bytes stop changing"],a:1,
e:"Caching is a **prefix** mechanism: any byte change invalidates everything from that point on, so a volatile block inside the cached region forces a full rewrite. The 1-hour option made this worse, because a 1-hour write costs **2x base input** (the 5-minute write costs 1.25x) and the entry is discarded 10 minutes later. Restructure by volatility: stable instructions and tools cached, the stock snapshot appended after the last breakpoint at ordinary input price. Principle: **the cache boundary is a stability boundary.**",
w:"A — a 5-minute write at 1.25x is cheaper per write but still rewrites the whole prefix every refresh; the layout is the defect. C — breakpoints mark where a cacheable prefix ends; they cannot protect content that sits behind changed bytes, because invalidation propagates forward. D — deterministic serialisation fixes accidental churn from key ordering, but this table's values genuinely change every 10 minutes."},

{id:1312,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A queue is placed in front of a Claude-backed service so that traffic bursts no longer produce 429s. What does the queue by itself fail to provide?",
o:["A. Ordering guarantees between concurrent requests","B. Protection against non-retryable 400 errors","C. Retry semantics, since queued jobs cannot be retried","D. Capacity - a queue converts overload into latency, so it must be paired with a bounded depth, admission control that rejects or defers excess work, and a caller-visible position or expected wait"],a:3,
e:"A queue smooths **arrival rate** against a fixed service rate. If arrivals exceed the rate limit for a sustained period, depth grows without bound and the burst is experienced as a 50-minute wait rather than a 429 - the failure has been renamed, not removed. A complete design adds **back-pressure**: a depth or age ceiling, an explicit shed-or-defer decision beyond it, and honest feedback to the caller. Principle: **queueing trades throughput for latency; only capacity or reduced demand fixes overload.**",
w:"A — ordering is a property of the queue you choose and is not the gap here. B — a 400 is a request defect; queueing neither causes nor prevents it, and such jobs should fail fast rather than be retried. C — queued jobs are entirely retryable; the queue is usually where retry and dead-letter handling live."},

{id:1313,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"To bound context growth, an agent application drops the oldest 20 entries from its stored `messages` array before each request. Some requests now fail with `400 invalid_request_error` referring to a `tool_use` id. What is the defect and the fix?",
o:["A. `tool_result` blocks may be sent without their originating `tool_use` provided the ids are omitted","B. The orphaned `tool_result` should be rewritten as a plain `user` text block and resent","C. The trimmed history now begins with an `assistant` turn, which is what the API rejects","D. Trimming by entry count can remove an assistant turn while leaving its matching `tool_result` behind; trim at whole-turn boundaries so each `tool_use` block and its matching `tool_result` are removed together"],a:3,
e:"Every `tool_use` block must have a `tool_result` whose `tool_use_id` matches it, and an **unmatched `tool_use` id is a 400**. Counting entries is blind to that pairing, so a fixed-size trim can decapitate a tool exchange. History pruning must operate on **semantically complete units** - a user turn, the assistant turn it produced including its `tool_use` blocks, and the `user` message carrying the corresponding `tool_result` blocks. The transferable principle: **conversation history is a structured transcript with referential integrity, not a list of strings.**",
w:"A — the id is what binds result to call; omitting it does not make the pair optional. B — rewriting a result as prose destroys the linkage the model needs and leaves the dangling `tool_use` unresolved. C — the rejection names a `tool_use` id; the leading role is a different concern, and consecutive same-role turns are merged rather than rejected."},

{id:1314,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"After moving a 40-second analysis to an async job, a worker holds the finished result in memory and pushes it over the open WebSocket. Mobile users on unstable networks frequently never receive their result and have to resubmit. What is missing?",
o:["A. A longer WebSocket keepalive interval so the socket survives mobile network changes","B. Durable storage: write the completed result under its job id, then notify, so a reconnecting client can fetch it by id","C. Automatic re-execution of the job whenever the client reconnects","D. Direct streaming from the Messages API to the mobile client so nothing is buffered server-side"],a:1,
e:"The defect is that the **result's lifetime is bound to a connection**. In an async design the job's output is a first-class record: persist it (with status, timestamps, and the job id the client already holds) before notifying anyone, and treat push as an optimisation over a `GET /jobs/{id}` the client can always fall back to. That also makes retries idempotent - a resubmitting client finds the existing job rather than paying for a second generation. Principle: **asynchronous work needs durable state, not a durable socket.**",
w:"A — keepalives cannot survive a handover, a tunnel change, or a backgrounded app; the result still exists only in one process's memory. C — re-running a 40-second generation on every reconnect doubles cost and can return a different answer to the same question. D — that reintroduces a client-held credential and still loses the result if the connection drops mid-stream."},

{id:1315,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A long-running assistant conversation is approaching the model's context window. Which design bounds context growth while preserving continuity?",
o:["A. Lower `max_tokens` on each request so the combined total stays under the window","B. Stop sending the system prompt once the history is long","C. Replace older turns with a compact running summary that the client stores and resends, keeping the most recent turns verbatim","D. Rely on the API to discard the oldest turns automatically once the window is reached"],a:2,
e:"Because the API is stateless, **history management is the client's job**. Rolling summarisation keeps the transcript bounded: older turns collapse into a summary block your application persists alongside the conversation, while recent turns stay verbatim so local reference and tool pairing survive. Note that the context window bounds the request **plus generated output including thinking tokens**, and overflow during generation surfaces as `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"` rather than silent truncation.",
w:"A — a smaller output cap frees a little headroom but does nothing about input that grows every turn, and it truncates answers. B — dropping the system prompt discards the instructions that define behaviour, and is a small saving compared with the history. D — nothing is discarded on your behalf; you either fit the window or receive an error."},

{id:1316,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"One tenant starts a 300,000-document bulk import through a shared service. Interactive tenants immediately see 429s and multi-second waits. Rate-limit headers show input-token capacity exhausted while request capacity remains. What is the right design change?",
o:["A. Give bulk work its own lane with per-tenant quotas: move the import to the Batch API and reserve a share of live capacity for interactive traffic","B. Raise `max_tokens` on interactive requests so they complete in fewer turns","C. Retry interactive requests immediately and bulk requests with backoff, so interactive traffic wins the race","D. Request a rate-limit increase, which is the only real remedy for shared-capacity contention"],a:0,
e:"Two workloads with different latency requirements are competing for one **shared token budget**. The structural fix is workload separation plus fairness: latency-tolerant bulk work belongs on the **Batch API**, which has its own processing path and a ~50% discount, and whatever remains live gets a per-tenant quota so no single tenant can consume the organisation's input-tokens-per-minute allowance. Principle: **isolate workloads by latency class before tuning retries.**",
w:"B — `max_tokens` is an output cap and does not factor into output-token rate limits at all; the exhausted dimension here is input tokens. C — retrying sooner adds load to an already throttled dimension and makes the contention worse. D — more headroom raises the ceiling both workloads share, so the next bulk import reproduces the incident."},

{id:1317,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent completes a task in six sequential tool-calling turns, taking about 34 seconds. It is exposed on a synchronous HTTP endpoint that times out at 30 seconds. The team lowers `max_tokens` from 8,000 to 2,000 hoping to speed it up; answers now truncate and it is still too slow. What is the correct analysis?",
o:["A. `max_tokens` should have been raised instead, since a larger cap lets the model finish in one turn","B. The endpoint is being rate-limited, and the 30-second boundary is a 429 in disguise","C. Latency is dominated by sequential model round trips and tool execution; move the run behind an async job with progress reporting, and consolidate or parallelise the tool hops. `max_tokens` caps output, it does not shorten the loop","D. Enabling streaming will bring the total under 30 seconds"],a:2,
e:"Six turns means six request-response cycles plus six tool executions; the wall clock is the **sum of the hops**, and no output cap changes that. Lowering `max_tokens` only introduced `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` truncation. The design answer is to stop pretending the work fits a synchronous budget: accept the request, return a job id, run the loop asynchronously, and report progress. Separately, reduce the hop count - consolidate tools, or issue independent calls in parallel and return all `tool_result` blocks in one `user` message.",
w:"A — a higher cap permits longer answers but cannot collapse six required tool round trips into one. B — a 429 carries an explicit `rate_limit_error` body and `retry-after`; a fixed 30-second cut-off is the gateway's timeout. D — streaming improves time-to-first-token and keeps connections alive, but the total work still takes about 34 seconds."},

{id:1318,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A grounded support assistant confidently quotes a refund window that was superseded three weeks ago. Investigation shows the cited passage really is in the retrieval index, which was last rebuilt five weeks ago. What is the correct fix?",
o:["A. Instruct the model in the system prompt to prefer the newest information available","B. Move to a model with a later training cut-off","C. Raise top-k so both the old and the new policy are retrieved and let the model choose","D. Fix the pipeline: re-index against a defined freshness SLA, stamp each chunk with an effective date, and remove or supersede retired versions so stale text is not retrievable"],a:3,
e:"The model answered correctly **from what it was given**; the defect is upstream, in the freshness of the grounding corpus. Knowledge freshness in a retrieval design is an operational property with three parts: an ingestion or re-index SLA that is monitored, effective-date metadata so recency can be reasoned about and filtered, and a retirement path so superseded documents leave the retrievable set. Principle: **a grounded assistant is exactly as current as its index, and index staleness is invisible in the model's output.**",
w:"A — the model cannot prefer text it never receives; the instruction has no effect on what retrieval returns. B — the answer came from your index, not from parametric memory, so the training cut-off is irrelevant here. C — retrieving both versions hands the model a contradiction with no authority to resolve it, and doubles the chance of citing the wrong one."},

{id:1319,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A streaming assistant fires a downstream \"create ticket\" webhook as soon as the streamed text contains the phrase `RAISE_TICKET`. Occasionally tickets are created for responses that end in `stop_reason: \"refusal\"`, or where the model went on to correct itself. What is the sound rule?",
o:["A. Match the phrase only after the `content_block_stop` for that block","B. Commit side effects only after the message completes and `stop_reason` has been inspected; the authoritative `stop_reason` arrives in `message_delta`, and partial text is not a decision","C. Add a stop sequence on the phrase so the model cannot revise itself afterwards","D. Watch `input_json_delta` fragments instead, since tool arguments are stable as they stream"],a:1,
e:"A stream is a **rendering channel, not a commit log**. Partial text may be revised by later blocks, and the turn may terminate in a way that invalidates everything shown - detect that via `stop_reason: \"refusal\"`, not by string matching. The final `stop_reason` and cumulative output usage arrive in **`message_delta`**, not `message_stop`, so that is the gate. Better still, express the action as a **tool call** so authorisation is a structured decision your code makes, rather than a phrase your code greps for.",
w:"A — a completed block is still not a completed message; a later block or a refusal can change the outcome. C — a stop sequence truncates the turn at the phrase, which guarantees you act on an unfinished response rather than preventing revision. D — `input_json_delta` carries `partial_json` fragments that must be concatenated per block `index` and parsed only when complete; individual fragments are not valid JSON."},

{id:1320,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"An application caches a large system prompt behind a `cache_control` breakpoint. It also builds its `tools` array by iterating a dictionary, so ordering varies between processes, and one route conditionally appends a debugging tool. What is the effect on caching?",
o:["A. Every breakpoint after the tools is invalidated: the cacheable prefix is matched in the order tools -> system -> messages and must be byte-identical","B. Only the tool definitions are re-billed; the cached system prompt still reads from cache","C. Tool definitions sit outside the cacheable prefix, so their ordering is irrelevant","D. Nothing changes, because `cache_control` matches semantically rather than byte-for-byte"],a:0,
e:"The cacheable prefix is assembled **tools -> system -> messages** and matched byte-for-byte from the start. Because tools come first, any churn there - non-deterministic ordering, a conditionally appended tool - changes the prefix and invalidates the system prompt and message blocks behind it. Two design rules follow: **serialise the tool set deterministically** (sorted, stable), and keep the tool set **fixed per route** rather than varying it per request. Remember that tool definitions are also billed as input tokens on every request, plus a fixed tool-use system-prompt overhead.",
w:"B — invalidation propagates forward through the prefix; content after changed bytes cannot read from cache. C — tools are the first segment of the cacheable prefix, which is precisely why ordering matters so much. D — matching is byte-identical; two semantically equivalent JSON serialisations with different key or element order are different prefixes."},

{id:1321,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A grounded assistant's vector store becomes unavailable while the Messages API is healthy. The service calls the model with an empty context block, and the assistant answers a policy question from general knowledge - incorrectly. What should the design do instead?",
o:["A. Detect the retrieval failure in code and return an explicit \"sources unavailable\" state without calling the model","B. Call the model anyway, adding \"answer only from the provided context\" to the system prompt","C. Fall back to the model's general knowledge and label such answers \"unverified\"","D. Block the request and retry the vector store until it recovers"],a:0,
e:"For a feature whose contract is *grounded answers*, the grounding source is a **hard dependency**: if it is unavailable, the feature is unavailable, and that must be enforced in deterministic code before the model is invoked. Calling the model with no context and hoping an instruction prevents an answer is the classic error - prompt instructions are advisory, and the model has no way to know that retrieval failed. Principle: **degrade by disabling the capability, not by silently lowering its guarantees.**",
w:"B — the instruction is advisory and, with an empty context, invites either a refusal or an ungrounded answer; the outcome is not controlled by you. C — a label does not restore the guarantee, and users read the answer rather than the disclaimer. D — blocking on an unhealthy dependency converts a clean degraded response into a hung request and a cascading failure."},

{id:1322,d:"D1",s:"Claude Application Design",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent takes about three minutes to complete a research task across several tools. The UI shows only a spinner, and a third of users cancel before it finishes. What is the appropriate design response?",
o:["A. Enable `\"stream\": true`, which is sufficient because the SSE events cover the whole run","B. Emit orchestration-level progress events from the agent loop to the client - step, tool name, elapsed time, interim findings - and support cancellation, rather than relying on model token streaming alone","C. Compress the work into a single request with all tools available in one turn","D. Display an estimated completion time derived from historical averages"],a:1,
e:"Token streaming makes **one model call** visible; an agent run is many calls interleaved with tool execution, and during tool work no model events are produced at all - so a stream alone still leaves long silent gaps. The interaction model for a multi-minute run is a **progress channel owned by the orchestrator**: publish step transitions, tool invocations, and partial results over SSE or a websocket, plus a cancel path that stops the loop and stops spending. Principle: **the unit of user-visible progress is your workflow step, not the model's token.**",
w:"A — each model call is its own stream, and the tool-execution intervals emit nothing, so the spinner gaps remain. C — a single turn cannot generally replace a genuine multi-step investigation, and forcing it degrades quality. D — an estimate is a useful garnish but it is not evidence of progress, and it does not let a user abandon the run."},

{id:1323,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A CI test asserts that a Claude-generated reply equals `\"Your balance is $42.00.\"`. It passes locally and fails roughly one run in five in CI, with identical inputs and the same pinned model. What should change?",
o:["A. Set `temperature: 0` in the test so the output becomes deterministic","B. Pin the model to a dated snapshot identifier so the weights cannot change","C. Raise `max_tokens` so the sentence is never truncated","D. Stop asserting exact generated strings: assert schema-level invariants and extracted values, and move quality judgements to a graded eval suite"],a:3,
e:"Generated prose is not a stable value to compare against. **Currency note:** `temperature`, `top_p` and `top_k` are deprecated and rejected with a **400** on current models, and even where sampling controls are still supported the output is **not fully deterministic at 0**. Assert what the contract actually requires: schema validity (ideally enforced with `output_config.format` and constrained decoding), the extracted numeric value, `stop_reason`, and the absence of forbidden content. Subjective quality belongs in a **separate graded eval suite** with a threshold, not in a unit test.",
w:"A — the parameter is deprecated and 400s on current models, and it never guaranteed byte-identical output. B — current model ids are already dateless pinned snapshots; pinning fixes the weights but serving infrastructure can change, so it gives stability rather than bit-identical text. C — truncation would show as `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`; an intermittent wording difference is not a length problem."},

{id:1324,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"To aid debugging, a claims service logs the full request and response JSON at INFO level into a shared platform that 200 engineers can query. The prompts contain customer names, dates of birth and claim narratives. What is the correct remedy?",
o:["A. Keep the logs as they are but shorten retention to 30 days","B. Log only the response, since the prompt is the sensitive half","C. Redact at the point of logging: emit `request-id`, model, `stop_reason`, `usage`, latency and content hashes, and keep any raw content in a separate access-controlled store with short retention","D. Encrypt the log files at rest, which satisfies the requirement"],a:2,
e:"**Redaction must happen before the log call**, not downstream, because once personal data is in a broadly readable index it has already been disclosed. Structured logging gives you nearly all the diagnostic value without the content: the **`request-id`** identifies the call to support, `model` and the prompt version identify the configuration, `stop_reason` distinguishes truncation and refusal from completion, and `usage` attributes cost. Where raw content is genuinely needed, sample it into a separate store with explicit access control, a short retention window and an audit trail.",
w:"A — a shorter window reduces the blast radius but the disclosure to 200 readers happens on day one. B — responses restate and elaborate on the personal data in the prompt; splitting the pair protects nothing. D — encryption at rest defends against stolen disks, not against the authorised readers who are the actual exposure here."},

{id:1325,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"After a route is switched to streaming, dashboards show `output_tokens` as 0 and `stop_reason` as \"unknown\" for every request. The logger records its metrics when it receives `message_stop`. What is wrong?",
o:["A. `usage` is only reported on non-streaming responses, so streamed calls cannot be instrumented","B. Read the final `stop_reason` and cumulative output usage from `message_delta`, which carries them; `message_stop` only terminates the stream","C. Sum the lengths of the `text_delta` fragments and divide by four to estimate the tokens","D. Call `/v1/messages/count_tokens` on the assembled response text afterwards"],a:1,
e:"In a streamed response the terminal accounting arrives in **`message_delta`**: that event carries the final `stop_reason` and the cumulative output token usage. `message_stop` is a bare end-of-stream marker. A logger hooked only to `message_stop` therefore records nothing, which is why the instrumentation silently zeroed out when the route changed shape. Use the SDK's final-message helper, or accumulate `message_delta` explicitly. Principle: **switching transport changes where your telemetry lives; instrument the stream, not just the request.**",
w:"A — usage is reported for streamed responses too; it simply arrives in a different event. C — characters-per-token varies by tokenizer and model (newer tokenizers produce roughly 30% more tokens for the same text), so a divide-by-four estimate is not billing data. D — `count_tokens` returns a single `input_tokens` estimate for messages, system and tools; it does not report what a completed response actually cost."},

{id:1326,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A service declares its Anthropic SDK dependency as `anthropic>=1.0` and commits no lockfile. Which risk does that create?",
o:["A. A rebuild can resolve a newer SDK release with changed types, defaults or behaviour, so an unrelated deploy can alter how the service behaves with no change of your own","B. The SDK will begin selecting a newer Claude model automatically","C. The `anthropic-version` API header will change automatically","D. Prompt caching stops working when SDK versions differ between environments"],a:0,
e:"An unpinned dependency makes your build a function of **when it ran**. The remedy is a committed lockfile plus deliberate, reviewed upgrades with the changelog read and the eval suite run. It also helps to keep the three independent version axes explicit: the **SDK package version**, the **`anthropic-version` request header**, and the **model id**. Conflating them is a common source of \"nothing changed but everything changed\" incidents.",
w:"B — the model is whatever your code passes in `model`; an SDK upgrade does not choose one for you. C — that header is a value your code sends; it moves only when you change it. D — caching is a server-side feature driven by `cache_control` in the request, not by client library parity."},

{id:1327,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"`anthropic-version` is hard-coded in 14 modules of a service, three of them with different values, and one newer module omits it and receives a 400. What is the sound remedy?",
o:["A. Remove the header everywhere and let the API default to its newest version","B. Keep it per call site so each feature can adopt new API behaviour independently","C. Set it in exactly one place - the internal client wrapper - resolved from configuration, so the whole process speaks one API version and bumping it is a single reviewed, tested change","D. Move it out of the headers and into the request body alongside `model`"],a:2,
e:"`anthropic-version` and `content-type: application/json` are **required headers**, and the version governs response shapes and defaults - so a process speaking three versions at once is a process with three behaviours. Centralising it in the client wrapper makes the version an explicit, greppable configuration value, gives you one place to bump under test, and eliminates the omission class of bug entirely. This is the same argument as wrapping the client itself: **one seam for every cross-cutting request concern.**",
w:"A — the header is required, not optional, and silently tracking the newest version is precisely the instability pinning exists to prevent. B — per-call-site divergence means a shared response parser must cope with several shapes at once, which is how the three different values arose. D — on the first-party API the version is a header; passing `anthropic_version` in the body is the Google Cloud request shape, not this one."}
,
{id:1328,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Unit tests use a hand-written stub that returns `{\"text\": \"...\"}`, and production code reads `response.content[0].text`. Tests stay green while production breaks whenever a response leads with a non-text block. What is the correct fix?",
o:["A. Delete the stubs and call the live API in unit tests","B. Assert on `response.content[0].text` in the tests as well, so tests and production code agree","C. Add a retry so a mismatched response shape is fetched again","D. Build fixtures from recorded real responses, type them with the SDK's response types, and iterate `content` by block type instead of indexing position 0"],a:3,
e:"A mock is a **contract claim about the real API**, and a hand-invented shape encodes a contract that does not exist - so the suite verifies the team's misconception. `content` is an ordered array of typed blocks (`text`, `tool_use`, and others depending on features in use), and position 0 is not guaranteed to be text. Deriving fixtures from recorded responses and typing them with the SDK's own types makes shape drift a compile-or-parse error instead of a production surprise. Principle: **mock the boundary faithfully or you have tested your assumptions, not your code.**",
w:"A — live calls in unit tests are slow, non-deterministic and costly; the answer is a faithful mock, not no mock. B — aligning the tests to the wrong shape entrenches the bug and removes the last chance of detecting it. C — the response is valid; your parser is wrong, so every retry returns the same correct shape."},

{id:1329,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"An internal wrapper around the Claude client is to expose one method to the rest of the codebase. Which values must it surface if callers are to detect truncation, attribute cost and raise a support ticket?",
o:["A. The concatenated response text and the HTTP status code","B. The response text and the wall-clock latency","C. The response text and the model id","D. The response text plus `stop_reason`, the `usage` object, the raw content blocks, and the `request-id` from the response headers"],a:3,
e:"A wrapper that returns only a string is a lossy abstraction. **`stop_reason`** is how you distinguish a complete answer from `max_tokens` truncation or a `refusal`; **`usage`** carries `input_tokens`, `output_tokens` and the cache counters needed for cost attribution; the **content blocks** are required for any tool use, since a `tool_use` block has no text; and **`request-id`** is the identifier Anthropic support needs for a specific failed call. Principle: **hide the transport, not the response's semantics.**",
w:"A — a 200 tells you the call succeeded, which is exactly the case where a truncated or refused response looks fine. B — latency is an operational metric and says nothing about completeness, cost or tool intent. C — the model id belongs in the log line but cannot reveal how the turn ended or what it consumed."},

{id:1330,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A customer disputes an answer the assistant gave six weeks ago. Prompts ship weekly, and the team cannot establish which prompt produced the answer. What practice would have prevented this?",
o:["A. Re-running the customer's question against the current prompt and comparing the two answers","B. Inferring the active prompt from the deployment timeline","C. Stamping every request record with the prompt version identifier alongside the model id and the parameter set, so any stored output maps back to the exact configuration that produced it","D. Keeping prompts in a database so past rows can be joined to outputs by timestamp"],a:2,
e:"Reproducibility for an LLM feature requires the **whole configuration**, not the code commit: prompt version, model id, `max_tokens`, `output_config` settings, the tool set, and the retrieval snapshot if there is one. Emitting that bundle as fields on each request record turns \"which prompt was live?\" into a lookup. Note that even with the bundle recorded you get **stability, not bit-identical replay** - a pinned model id fixes the weights, while serving infrastructure can change - so the record is evidence of configuration, not a guarantee of the same text.",
w:"A — today's prompt is a different system, so the comparison cannot establish what happened six weeks ago. B — deployment timestamps are approximate, ignore staged rollouts and flags, and break entirely when a variant test was running. D — timestamp joins are fragile across rollouts and time zones, and moving prompt text into a database also removes it from code review and diffs."},

{id:1331,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A team's service is written in a language with no official Anthropic SDK. What is the sound approach?",
o:["A. Call `POST /v1/messages` over HTTPS with the required headers, implementing retries, backoff and SSE parsing deliberately - or isolate the API calls in a small service written in one of the supported languages","B. Use an OpenAI-compatible shim so one client library serves every provider","C. Wait for an SDK, because raw HTTP access is unsupported","D. Shell out to a command-line tool for each request"],a:0,
e:"There are **seven official SDKs** - Python, TypeScript, C#, Go, Java, PHP and Ruby - and the API itself is plain HTTP: `POST /v1/messages` with `anthropic-version`, `content-type: application/json` and a credential header (`Authorization: Bearer <token>` or the legacy `x-api-key`). Raw HTTP is entirely supported; what you take on is the work the SDKs already do - typed errors, retry and backoff policy, streaming event assembly, and pagination. Isolating those calls behind one small supported-language service is often the cheaper trade.",
w:"B — a translation shim hides the features that matter (content blocks, `stop_reason`, cache controls, structured outputs) behind a lowest-common-denominator surface. C — the HTTP API is the documented interface; SDKs are conveniences over it. D — process-per-request is fragile, hard to instrument, and cannot stream sensibly."},

{id:1332,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A service keeps the SDK client's default retry behaviour, adds a decorator that retries five times, and runs inside a job runner that retries three times. During a spell of 529s one logical request occupies a worker for many minutes and the provider sees a surge of traffic. What is the correct structure?",
o:["A. Keep all three layers but shorten each delay so the total stays bounded","B. Own retries at exactly one layer, with jitter and a total wall-clock budget, recognising that a retried timeout means elapsed time can reach the timeout multiplied by the number of attempts","C. Disable retries entirely and surface every transient error to the user","D. Move retries into the API gateway so they are centralised, while keeping the SDK's defaults as a safety net"],a:1,
e:"Nested retry layers **multiply**: the attempt count is the product of the layers, and because each attempt can consume the full timeout, worst-case wall clock is the timeout times the attempts. That both ties up workers and adds load to an already degraded service. Choose one layer - typically the SDK's built-in policy, or your own with the SDK's disabled - add **jitter** so retries do not synchronise, and cap the total elapsed budget. Principle: **retry is a cross-cutting policy; it must live in exactly one place.**",
w:"A — shorter delays remove the backoff that lets a degraded service recover, turning multiplication into a tight hammering loop. C — 429, 500 and 529 are genuinely transient and are the cases retries exist for; removing them trades one failure mode for another. D — keeping the SDK defaults underneath a gateway retry recreates the multiplication the question describes."},

{id:1333,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"How should exception handling around a Claude API client be structured?",
o:["A. A single broad handler for all API errors, with two retries","B. Matching on the error message text, so newly introduced error types are covered automatically","C. A most-specific-first chain of typed exceptions, so 400-class failures fail fast and increment an alert metric while 429 and 5xx follow the backoff path","D. A circuit breaker around the client, which removes the need to distinguish status codes"],a:2,
e:"One broad handler destroys the only distinction that matters operationally: **your bug versus their weather**. 400 `invalid_request_error`, 401 `authentication_error`, 403 `permission_error` and 413 `request_too_large` are deterministic - retrying them wastes quota and hides the defect, so they should fail fast and raise an alert. 429 `rate_limit_error` (honouring `retry-after`), 500 `api_error` and 529 `overloaded_error` are the retryable set. A most-specific-first chain of the SDK's typed exceptions expresses that policy in code.",
w:"A — a shared handler retries non-retryable client errors and buries the signal that something is misconfigured. B — message strings are not an API contract and change without notice; the typed exceptions and status codes are the stable surface. D — a breaker limits blast radius during an outage but says nothing about whether a given failure should ever be retried."},

{id:1334,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A codebase defines its own `interface ChatMessage { role: string; content: string }` and maps every API response into it. What does that break?",
o:["A. Nothing, provided the mapping function is unit tested","B. Any response whose `content` is not a single text block - `tool_use` and other block types are flattened or lost, and the compiler can no longer catch the mismatch","C. Prompt caching, because the SDK can no longer see the original message objects","D. Streaming, because deltas cannot be typed"],a:1,
e:"`content` is an **array of typed content blocks**, not a string, on both requests and responses. Collapsing it to `string` throws away tool calls, images, and multi-block answers, and - worse - moves the loss out of the type system so the failure appears at runtime in production. The SDKs export types for every API object (message params, tools, content blocks, responses); using them makes shape changes a compile error. Principle: **do not redefine the vendor's data model; import it.**",
w:"A — the mapper can be perfectly tested and still be lossy by design, because the target type cannot represent a `tool_use` block. C — caching is driven by `cache_control` in the request payload and is unaffected by your internal types. D — streaming deltas are typed by the SDK; the problem here is the collapse of the response model, not the stream."},

{id:1335,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A TypeScript service sets `timeout: 30` on its Anthropic client, intending 30 seconds. Long-generation requests fail almost immediately, while a Python worker configured with `timeout=30` behaves as expected. What is the cause?",
o:["A. The TypeScript client's `timeout` is expressed in milliseconds while Python's is in seconds, so the value meant 30 ms","B. The TypeScript SDK does not support a client-level timeout, only per-request options","C. Streaming requests ignore the client timeout, so only the non-streaming route is affected","D. The default retry policy consumed the time budget before the first attempt was made"],a:0,
e:"Timeout **units differ between SDKs**: TypeScript takes milliseconds, Python and Ruby take seconds, Go takes a duration, Java a `Duration`, C# a `TimeSpan`. A bare `30` therefore means very different things in a polyglot codebase. Two defences: name the configuration key with its unit (`REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS`) and convert at the edge, and remember that timeouts are retried - so worst-case wall clock is the timeout multiplied by the attempt count. The default client timeout is generous (about 10 minutes), so an instant failure points at a unit error, not a slow model.",
w:"B — a client-level timeout is supported, and per-request overrides exist as well. C — a stream would not fail instantly from an unrelated setting; the failure tracks the misread value on every request shape. D — retries consume time after an attempt fails; they cannot pre-empt the first attempt."},

{id:1336,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A test suite patches the SDK's internal HTTP transport to fake responses. A routine SDK upgrade breaks 240 tests, though no production code changed. What is the sound test architecture?",
o:["A. Pin the SDK permanently so its internals never move","B. Replay recorded HTTP traffic in every test, which removes all coupling to the library","C. Move the affected tests to the live API so no faking is required","D. Mock at the seam you own - your internal client interface - so the SDK's internals are exercised only by a handful of tests around the wrapper itself"],a:3,
e:"Tests should depend on **contracts you control**. Patching a library's private transport couples 240 tests to an implementation detail that the vendor is free to change, which is why an upgrade with no behavioural impact produced mass failure. Wrapping the client behind an internal interface gives you one seam: application tests fake that interface, and a small number of integration tests around the wrapper cover the real request and response shapes. Principle: **the blast radius of a dependency upgrade should be proportional to how much of your code touches it.**",
w:"A — a permanent pin trades a fixable test-design problem for accumulating security and compatibility debt. B — record-and-replay is a legitimate technique for the wrapper's own tests, but it is still coupled to wire details and cannot remove all coupling as claimed. C — live calls make the suite slow, costly and non-deterministic, and they still would not have survived the upgrade any better."},

{id:1337,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A buffered code path is thoroughly tested. The streaming path intermittently fails to parse tool arguments, more often when the arguments are large. What is the defect and how should it be covered?",
o:["A. Each `input_json_delta` carries only a fragment in `partial_json`; concatenate every fragment for that block `index` and parse once, after `content_block_stop`. Cover it with recorded event fixtures including fragment boundaries","B. Parse each `partial_json` fragment as it arrives and merge the resulting objects","C. Tool arguments are only available on non-streaming responses, so the streaming path must re-issue the request","D. Set `disable_parallel_tool_use` so only one tool block streams at a time and fragments cannot interleave"],a:0,
e:"Tool arguments stream as **`input_json_delta` events whose `partial_json` fragments are not individually valid JSON** - a large argument is simply split across more of them, which is why size correlates with failure. The assembler must accumulate per block **`index`** and parse once the block closes. Because this logic is invisible on the buffered path, it needs its own tests built from recorded event sequences, with fragments split at awkward points. Principle: **stream assembly is application logic and must be unit tested like any other parser.**",
w:"B — a fragment such as `{\"que` cannot be parsed or merged; only the concatenation is valid JSON. C — streaming fully supports tool use; re-issuing the request doubles cost to work around a parser bug. D — that flag limits the model to one tool call per turn; it does not change how a single argument's fragments arrive."},

{id:1338,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which structural choice makes prompt construction the easiest part of a Claude integration to test?",
o:["A. Building the prompt inside the same function that performs the API call, so the two cannot drift apart","B. Persisting each assembled prompt to the database before it is sent","C. Asserting on the model's reply for a known input","D. Making prompt assembly a pure function of its inputs that returns the request payload, so it can be asserted on with no network involved"],a:3,
e:"Separating **assembly** from **I/O** turns the most change-prone part of the integration into ordinary, fast, deterministic unit tests: given this user, tenant and retrieved context, the payload contains these blocks, in this order, with the breakpoint here, under this token budget. It also makes cache-prefix stability testable, since you can assert that two calls with different volatile inputs produce an identical prefix. Principle: **push non-determinism to the edges and keep the core pure.**",
w:"A — fusing them means every prompt assertion needs the network faked, and coupling does not prevent drift; tests do. B — persistence is an audit measure, not a test, and it happens after the code you want to verify has already run. C — the reply is generated text and is not a stable assertion target; that belongs in a graded eval suite."},

{id:1339,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A pull request adds nine tool definitions and a 5,000-token few-shot block to a high-traffic route. Nobody notices until the monthly invoice. Which control catches this at review time?",
o:["A. Estimating tokens in the test as characters divided by four","B. Running the route in staging with `max_tokens: 1` and reading the bill the following day","C. A CI gate that assembles the request payload and calls `/v1/messages/count_tokens`, failing the build when a route exceeds its declared input-token budget","D. Setting `defer_loading: true` on every tool so definitions stop being sent"],a:2,
e:"`/v1/messages/count_tokens` is **free**, has its own rate limit independent of message creation, requires no `max_tokens`, and returns a single **`input_tokens`** figure covering messages, system and tools - which makes it an ideal build-time assertion. Declaring a per-route input budget converts an invisible cost regression into a failed check, and it also catches the less obvious half: **tool definitions are billed as input tokens on every request**, plus a fixed tool-use system-prompt overhead.",
w:"A — characters-per-token is tokenizer- and model-specific, and newer tokenizers produce roughly 30% more tokens for the same text, so the estimate is not a budget. B — a next-day invoice is not a review-time control, and `max_tokens: 1` measures nothing about input size. D — at least one tool must have `defer_loading: false` or the request 400s, and deferred definitions are still transmitted in `tools` on every request."},

{id:1340,d:"D1",s:"Software Engineering Foundations",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An eval golden set consists of 400 real customer transcripts committed to a repository that is cloned to contractors' laptops. What is the right resolution?",
o:["A. Move the golden set out of version control into the runtime configuration service","B. De-identify before committing: synthesise or pseudonymise the cases, keep the small residue of genuinely real data in an access-controlled store outside the repository, and keep the de-identified set in version control where it can be reviewed and diffed","C. Encrypt the fixture file in the repository and share the key with the team","D. Replace the golden set entirely with model-generated cases"],a:1,
e:"An eval suite is a **code artefact**: it belongs in version control so changes are reviewed and diffed, and so a score can be attributed to a commit. That is precisely why it must not carry personal data - a repository is copied, mirrored and retained indefinitely. De-identify at the point of curation, keep the handful of cases that genuinely need real data in a controlled store referenced by id, and preserve the distribution (lengths, edge cases, awkward inputs) so the set still resembles production.",
w:"A — the configuration store is neither reviewed nor diffed, so this trades the privacy problem for loss of the eval's version history. C — a committed encrypted blob with a shared key is functionally shared data, and it is still in the history forever. D — purely synthetic cases lose the real distribution of messy inputs, which is the main value of a golden set."},

{id:1341,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"So that product managers can edit prompts without a deploy, a team moves all prompt text into a database table edited through an admin screen. Three weeks later output quality has visibly dropped and nobody can say what changed. What is the sound arrangement?",
o:["A. Add an audit log to the admin screen, which restores the missing history","B. Keep prompt text in version control with review and eval gates, and let configuration select which reviewed version is active rather than carry the text itself","C. Give each product manager their own prompt row so edits cannot collide","D. Cache prompts in the application so database edits take effect only after a restart"],a:1,
e:"Prompt text **is behaviour**, so it belongs where behaviour belongs: in version control, reviewed, diffable, and gated by the eval suite. What genuinely wants to be runtime configuration is the **selector** - which reviewed prompt version is active for which tenant or environment - because that is what you need to flip during an incident without a deploy. This split keeps both properties: no-deploy switching, and no unreviewed behaviour change. Principle: **configuration chooses between reviewed artefacts; it should not become an unreviewed artefact.**",
w:"A — an audit log records who typed what, but the change still bypasses review, diffs and the eval gate, so quality regressions still ship unexamined. C — collision avoidance addresses a problem the team does not have; the problem is unreviewed behaviour change. D — a restart requirement removes the only benefit of the arrangement while keeping all of its drawbacks."},

{id:1342,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"The same setting is defined as a code default, in a config file, and as an environment variable. What must the configuration layer guarantee?",
o:["A. That the code default always wins, so behaviour is reproducible","B. That the config file wins, because it is the version-controlled source","C. A single documented precedence order, validated at startup, with the effective resolved values logged so an operator can see what the process is actually using","D. That every value is present in all three sources"],a:2,
e:"Multi-source configuration is only safe when precedence is **documented, implemented in one place, and observable**. Logging the resolved effective values at startup - with credentials redacted - collapses a whole class of incidents where staging and production differ for reasons nobody can see. Pair it with validation: required, behaviour-affecting keys must be present and well-typed, or the process refuses to start. Principle: **you cannot operate a system whose effective configuration you cannot read.**",
w:"A — that inverts the usual and useful order, in which deployment-specific sources override baked-in defaults. B — a version-controlled file cannot carry per-environment credentials or endpoints, which is exactly what environment variables are for. D — requiring every key everywhere defeats the purpose of layering and multiplies the places a change must be made."},

{id:1343,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A new production environment was created without the `MODEL_ID` variable. The code read it with a fallback to an older model id, so production quietly ran a different model for nine days while quality metrics drifted. What is the correct practice?",
o:["A. Add the missing variable and move on, since the fallback prevented an outage","B. Change the fallback to the newest available model instead of an older one","C. Discover the model id at startup from the API's model listing, so it is always valid","D. Treat behaviour-affecting settings as required: validate them at startup and refuse to boot when they are missing, and record the effective model id on every request log"],a:3,
e:"A silent default for a behaviour-affecting setting converts a **configuration error into a slow quality incident**, which is far harder to diagnose than a failed boot. The rule is a two-tier split: connection details may have sensible defaults, but anything that changes what the system produces - model id, prompt version, `max_tokens`, effort - is required and validated at startup. Recording the effective model id on every request closes the loop, so the drift would have been one dashboard facet away.",
w:"A — the fallback caused the incident by masking the error; leaving the pattern in place guarantees a repeat. B — defaulting to the newest model is still an unannounced behaviour change, and it also breaks the pinning that keeps production stable. C — a live listing tells you which ids exist, not which one this service was designed and evaluated against, and it is unavailable on some deployment platforms."},

{id:1344,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A production A/B test compares two system prompts. What property must variant assignment have?",
o:["A. Deterministic assignment by hashing a stable unit - user or conversation id - together with the experiment name, with the assigned variant recorded on every request and outcome record","B. A fresh random draw per request, seeded per process so the split is even","C. Alternation by request counter, so the arms receive exactly equal volume","D. Assignment by the parity of the current second, which is cheap and unbiased"],a:0,
e:"Assignment must be **deterministic per unit and stable for the life of the experiment**, or the same user meets both prompts in one session - which is a bad experience and destroys attribution. Hashing a stable identifier together with the experiment name gives stickiness with no shared state, and lets you run several experiments independently. The second half is equally load-bearing: **record the variant id** on the request and on every downstream outcome, or you cannot compare the arms afterwards.",
w:"B — a per-request draw makes a single conversation switch tone mid-way and makes per-user outcome analysis impossible. C — a counter is not sticky per user and correlates with concurrency, so arms drift apart under load. D — clock parity correlates with traffic patterns and batch jobs, and it is not sticky per user either."},

{id:1345,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Two system-prompt variants of about 6,000 tokens each, roughly 90% identical, are put behind an A/B test. `cache_creation_input_tokens` roughly doubles and the hit rate halves. What is the correct plumbing?",
o:["A. Give each variant its own cache breakpoint, since four are available per request","B. Move both arms to the 1-hour cache TTL so entries survive longer","C. Run the two arms on different models so their caches do not compete","D. Split the prompt so the identical 90% forms one shared, byte-identical cached prefix, with only the differing tail after the final breakpoint"],a:3,
e:"Each distinct prefix is a distinct cache entry, so two near-identical 6,000-token prompts create two entries and two streams of writes at **1.25x base input**, while reads would have cost **0.1x**. Restructuring so the shared 90% is one byte-identical prefix means both arms read the same warm entry and only the small variant tail is uncached. This is the general shape of A/B plumbing for prompts: **vary the smallest possible suffix**, and keep the variant text out of the shared prefix.",
w:"A — breakpoints mark where prefixes end; they cannot make two different byte sequences share one entry. B — a 1-hour write costs 2x base input, so with two full prefixes still being written this makes the bill worse. C — caches are model-scoped anyway, and changing the model between arms confounds the experiment with a second variable."},

{id:1346,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Staging sets `max_tokens: 1024` to save money while production uses 8192. Truncation defects - responses ending mid-sentence with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` - are only ever found in production. What is the correct policy?",
o:["A. Keep behaviour-affecting parameters identical across environments and differ only in credentials, endpoints and traffic volume; reduce staging cost by sending fewer requests rather than by changing `max_tokens`","B. Raise staging's `max_tokens` above the model's ceiling so it can never truncate","C. Add a staging-only retry whenever `stop_reason` is `max_tokens`","D. Test truncation only in production, behind a flag"],a:0,
e:"An environment that differs in a **behaviour-affecting parameter is not a rehearsal** of production - it exercises a different system. Environment configuration should vary the things that must vary (credentials, base URLs, quotas, log verbosity) and hold constant the things that shape output: model id, prompt version, `max_tokens`, effort, tool set. Where cost is the concern, reduce **volume**, not fidelity.",
w:"B — a `max_tokens` above the model's ceiling is rejected with a **400**; there is no silent clamping. C — a retry hides the symptom in the one environment where you wanted to see it. D — deferring the discovery to production is precisely the outcome the team is trying to avoid."},

{id:1347,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"During an incident an operator flips the runtime model-id flag to a different tier. Requests succeed, but cost per request rises sharply and `cache_read_input_tokens` is 0. What explains this?",
o:["A. The new model rejects `cache_control`, so caching is disabled until the flag is reverted","B. Caches are model-scoped, so the switch starts from a cold cache and early requests pay writes at 1.25x base input instead of reads at 0.1x","C. The 5-minute TTL expired during the incident and cannot be re-established while load is high","D. Changing the flag altered the prefix bytes, so the cache is permanently invalid"],a:1,
e:"Prompt caches are **scoped to the model**, so a model switch is always a cold start: the first requests on the new id pay cache writes, then reads resume as the prefix warms (the 5-minute TTL refreshes on each read). This is expected and temporary, and it belongs in the runbook so the operator is not surprised into reverting a working mitigation. For a planned switch you can pre-warm the new model's prefix before shifting traffic.",
w:"A — `cache_control` is supported across current models; the entries simply do not carry over between them. C — TTL expiry is per entry and re-establishes on the next write; it does not explain a persistent zero on a newly selected model. D — nothing is permanently invalid; the prefix is unchanged, it is merely not yet cached for this model."},

{id:1348,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A service's configuration file has carried `temperature: 0.2` and `top_p: 0.9` since 2024. After the model id is updated to a current model, every request returns `400 invalid_request_error`. What is the correct change?",
o:["A. Clamp `temperature` into the 0.0-1.0 range and retry","B. Set `temperature: 1.0`, the documented default, so the value becomes a no-op","C. Remove the deprecated sampling parameters - they are rejected with a 400 on current models - and express the intent through `output_config.effort` plus prompt instructions","D. Move the two parameters from the configuration file into the request body"],a:2,
e:"**Currency note:** `temperature`, `top_p` and `top_k` are deprecated and **rejected with a 400** on models after Opus 4.6; steering is now done with `output_config.effort` (`low`/`medium`/`high`/`xhigh`/`max`) and with prompting. Where they are still accepted, `temperature` ranges 0.0-1.0 with a default of 1.0 and output is not fully deterministic even at 0. The wider lesson for configuration management: a parameter set is **part of the model-migration diff**, not inert data - migrating a model id without auditing the parameters is how a config file becomes a landmine.",
w:"A — the value is already inside the legacy range; the rejection is about the parameter existing at all on this model. B — the default value is still an explicitly supplied deprecated parameter, so it still 400s. D — the location of the setting is irrelevant; it reaches the same request field either way."},

{id:1349,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An API credential is read once into a module-level constant at import time. A scheduled security rotation invalidates it and every pod returns `401 authentication_error` until it is redeployed. What is the correct arrangement?",
o:["A. Resolve credentials through a refreshable provider rather than an import-time constant, and re-resolve once on a 401 before failing the request","B. Bake the credential into the container image so rotation becomes a rebuild and redeploy","C. Keep two credentials in the environment and alternate between them per request","D. Catch the 401 and retry with backoff until the credential works again"],a:0,
e:"`401 authentication_error` is **not retryable without changing the credential**, so the only remedy is to be able to fetch a new one at runtime. Read the secret through a provider that can refresh - a secret manager client, an instance identity, or the SDK's own credential resolution - and treat a single 401 as a signal to re-resolve before surfacing the error. Keep credential handling in the one client wrapper: both `Authorization: Bearer <token>` and the legacy `x-api-key` header are valid, so a rotation may even change the header form.",
w:"B — baking secrets into images spreads them into a registry and makes every rotation a full deploy, which is the problem restated. C — alternating credentials doubles the exposure without guaranteeing either is current after a rotation. D — backoff retries of a revoked credential will fail forever while consuming worker time; the credential must change, not the timing."},

{id:1350,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A service has a classification route generating about 256 output tokens and a report route generating about 30,000. How should request timeouts and retries be configured?",
o:["A. Raise a single global timeout to 15 minutes so every route fits comfortably","B. Configure timeout and retry policy per route, sized to each route's expected generation time, and stream the long-generation route so the connection stays active","C. Lower `max_tokens` on the report route until it completes inside the classification route's timeout","D. Retry the report route more times, so one attempt eventually completes within the timeout"],a:1,
e:"Timeout is a **per-route property**, because it encodes an expectation about how long the work should take. One global value is either too tight for long generations or so loose that a genuinely hung fast route ties up a worker for minutes. Very large outputs should also be **streamed**, which keeps data flowing and avoids a single long silent wait; SDKs commonly require streaming for the largest `max_tokens` values for exactly this reason. Keep in mind that timeouts are retried, so the total wall clock is the timeout multiplied by the attempt count.",
w:"A — a 15-minute ceiling on the classification route means a stuck request is indistinguishable from a slow one for a quarter of an hour. C — truncating the product to fit an arbitrary infrastructure setting inverts the priority; the setting exists to serve the requirement. D — every attempt faces the same ceiling, so more attempts multiply cost and latency without changing the outcome."},

{id:1351,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Tenants may supply free-text \"custom instructions\" through a self-service configuration screen. One tenant pastes a 60,000-token handbook; that tenant's latency and cost per request triple and some requests begin to fail. What should the configuration layer do?",
o:["A. Accept the value and bill the tenant for the difference","B. Silently truncate the instruction to the first 2,000 tokens","C. Validate tenant-supplied configuration at save time against a schema with a hard token budget measured by `count_tokens`, reject over-budget values with an actionable error, and position the value after the shared cached prefix","D. Move the instruction into the first `user` message so it is no longer counted as input"],a:2,
e:"Tenant-supplied configuration is **input**, and input is validated at the boundary. Validate at **save** time, not request time, so the tenant gets an immediate, actionable error rather than a slow degradation in production: check the shape against a schema, measure the token cost with the free `/v1/messages/count_tokens` endpoint, and enforce a documented ceiling. Placement matters too - putting per-tenant text after the shared cached prefix preserves cache reuse for every other tenant.",
w:"A — accepting unbounded configuration makes one tenant's paste a latency and reliability problem for the platform, and billing does not fix latency. B — silent truncation drops half a policy without telling anyone, producing confidently wrong answers. D — every token in the request is billed as input regardless of which field carries it, and moving it changes nothing about the size."},

{id:1352,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A newly shipped tool that issues customer refunds must be disablable within a minute during an incident. Today, disabling it requires a code change and a 25-minute pipeline. What should be added?",
o:["A. A system-prompt instruction telling the model not to use the tool during incidents","B. An operational step that sets `tool_choice: {\"type\": \"none\"}` on all requests for the duration","C. Revocation of the downstream refund service's credentials","D. A runtime flag evaluated per request that both removes the tool from the `tools` array and hard-blocks its executor, defaulting to disabled if the flag store cannot be read"],a:3,
e:"A kill switch has to act in the layer that actually performs the action. Removing the tool from `tools` stops the model proposing it, and blocking the executor stops any in-flight or replayed call from executing - **the model proposes, deterministic code authorises**. Defaulting to the safe state when the flag store is unreachable makes the switch fail closed. Accept that changing the `tools` array invalidates the cached prefix; during an incident that is a trivial price.",
w:"A — prompt instructions are advisory and cannot be relied on as a control; they also do nothing about a replayed or in-flight call. B — `{\"type\": \"none\"}` does prevent tool calls, but it disables every tool at once and leaves the executor path open to anything already queued. C — revoking downstream credentials is a blunt last resort that breaks every other consumer of that service and produces confusing errors."},

{id:1353,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Where should the JSON Schemas used for `output_config.format` and for tool `input_schema` live?",
o:["A. In the runtime configuration store, so they can be changed without a deploy","B. Inline at each call site, so each feature owns its own copy","C. In version control alongside the application code, with tests, because a schema is a contract: changing it changes what callers must handle and changes the cached prefix bytes","D. In the database, versioned per row, so historical outputs can be validated against historical schemas"],a:2,
e:"A schema is **code-shaped**: `output_config.format` with `{\"type\":\"json_schema\",\"schema\":{...}}` guarantees schema-valid output through constrained decoding, and the tool-side equivalent is `strict: true` on the tool definition - so the schema determines both what the model may emit and what your parser must accept. Changing it is an interface change that wants review, tests and a coordinated deploy. It also sits inside the cacheable prefix (tools are rendered first), so edits invalidate cached content.",
w:"A — a no-deploy schema change breaks the deployed parser that consumes it, and it escapes review entirely. B — duplicated inline schemas drift apart, and identical-looking copies with different key order produce different cache prefixes. D — per-row history is a useful audit record, but it does not make the database the right home for the live contract."},

{id:1354,d:"D1",s:"Configuration Management",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A service enables two beta features by sending two separate `anthropic-beta` headers, and its configuration also lists a beta name the organisation is not entitled to. What is the outcome and the correct shape?",
o:["A. Repeated headers are merged by the API, and an unentitled name is ignored","B. An unentitled name returns `403 permission_error`, while repeated headers are valid","C. Repeated headers are valid, but an unentitled name silently disables both features","D. Multiple betas belong in a single comma-separated `anthropic-beta` header, and an invalid or unentitled beta name returns `400 invalid_request_error`"],a:3,
e:"Betas are declared in **one comma-separated `anthropic-beta` header** (`feature1,feature2`), not as repeated headers, and an invalid or unentitled name is a **request validation failure - 400 `invalid_request_error`**, not a permission error and not a silent no-op. For configuration management this means the beta list is a single validated configuration value: build it in one place, keep it under review, and remember that removing a beta can change response shapes just as adding one can.",
w:"A — repeated headers are not the documented shape, and an unentitled name is rejected rather than ignored. B — the failure is a 400 validation error; 403 `permission_error` covers a different class of problem. C — nothing is silent here: the request fails outright, which is preferable because it is visible."}
,
{id:1355,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A stakeholder writes that the chat assistant must feel \"real-time\". Answers typically run to about 2,000 tokens. What is the right way to make this testable?",
o:["A. Specify p95 time-to-first-token and p95 time-to-completion as separate targets, because streaming decouples perceived responsiveness from total generation time","B. Specify an average end-to-end response time, which is what users actually experience","C. Specify a maximum `max_tokens` so no response can run long","D. Specify a p99 end-to-end target only, since averages hide the bad cases"],a:0,
e:"\"Real-time\" conflates two independent quantities. **Time-to-first-token** is what a streaming UI can make small - often well under a second - and it is what users read as responsiveness. **Time-to-completion** for 2,000 tokens is several seconds no matter what, and it is what matters for a machine consumer or a copy-paste workflow. Separating them makes the requirement testable and tells you which design lever applies: streaming for the first, model tier, effort and output length for the second.",
w:"B — an average conceals the tail that generates complaints, and it still merges the two distinct quantities into one number. C — capping output length changes the product to satisfy a metric, and it introduces `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` truncation. D — a p99 target is a sensible addition, but one combined figure still cannot express that first-token latency and completion latency have different targets."},

{id:1356,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A requirement states that the system must \"handle our peak load\". Which quantities must it name for the capacity plan to be checkable against the API's limits?",
o:["A. Concurrent users and average response length","B. Requests per minute, input tokens per minute and output tokens per minute, because rate limits are enforced independently on those three dimensions","C. Requests per second and total monthly spend","D. Peak concurrency and `max_tokens`, since `max_tokens` reserves output capacity"],a:1,
e:"Rate limits apply on **three dimensions at once**: requests per minute, **input** tokens per minute and **output** tokens per minute. A plan expressed only in requests can exhaust the input-token dimension at a fraction of the request ceiling - a common surprise for document-heavy workloads. The `anthropic-ratelimit-*` response headers report remaining capacity per dimension, and `retry-after` accompanies a 429, so the requirement should be stated in the same units the limits and headers use.",
w:"A — concurrency and average length are inputs to the calculation, not the quantities the limits are expressed in. C — spend is a budget constraint; it does not tell you whether a minute of peak traffic fits within the throttles. D — `max_tokens` does **not** factor into output-token rate limits, so it reserves nothing and lowering it relieves no throttling."},

{id:1357,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A successful single-department pilot is asked to \"scale to all 40 departments\". Which clarification most changes the architecture?",
o:["A. Whether the current model tier can absorb 40 times the traffic","B. Whether departments must be isolated from one another's data, carry different policies, and be billed separately - the difference between more traffic and a multi-tenant system","C. Whether the interface can be rebranded per department","D. Whether the retrieval index should be sharded by department"],a:1,
e:"\"Scale\" is ambiguous between **volume** and **multi-tenancy**, and they lead to different systems. More traffic is a capacity question: throughput, rate limits, queueing. Isolation, per-department policy and separate cost attribution are structural: a tenant identity derived from the authenticated session, per-tenant configuration and prompt selection, per-tenant quotas so one department cannot starve another, and cost attribution on every request. Establishing which is meant determines whether this is a scaling exercise or a redesign.",
w:"A — capacity is a real question but it is answered with quotas and queueing, not with a different architecture. C — branding is presentation and can be added at any point without structural consequence. D — index sharding is one possible consequence of the isolation answer, not the requirement that decides it."},

{id:1358,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A fraud-screening assistant is specified only as needing to be \"accurate\". A missed fraudulent claim costs roughly 9,000 pounds; a false alarm costs about 40 pounds of analyst time plus customer annoyance. Which question most changes the design?",
o:["A. What false-negative rate is acceptable, or what the relative cost of the two error types is, since that fixes the decision threshold and the volume of human review the design must support","B. What overall accuracy percentage the stakeholder expects, since that is what the requirement means","C. Which model tier is affordable, since accuracy follows capability","D. What request volume to expect, since the design follows throughput"],a:0,
e:"With a cost ratio of roughly 200 to 1, a single \"accuracy\" figure is meaningless - the design should deliberately trade many cheap false alarms for very few expensive misses. Eliciting the acceptable **false-negative rate** (or the cost ratio) fixes three things at once: where the escalation threshold sits, how much analyst capacity must exist to absorb the alarms, and what the eval suite must measure - per-class recall rather than aggregate accuracy. Principle: **asymmetric error costs are a requirement, and they belong in the specification.**",
w:"B — aggregate accuracy is optimised by the trivial \"never flag anything\" model when fraud is rare; it is the wrong objective here. C — capability shifts the whole curve but cannot decide where on it you want to sit, which is a business decision. D — volume determines capacity and cost, not the operating point or the review policy."},

{id:1359,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A stakeholder states: \"the model must run inside our network; no data may leave it.\" Which clarification most determines feasibility?",
o:["A. Which cloud vendor the organisation already uses","B. Whether the team will accept a pricing premium for a region-pinned endpoint","C. Whether the corporate proxy can pass server-sent events","D. Whether \"inside our network\" means running model weights on their own hardware, or means a contractual and network data-path boundary with a defined residency region - because only the second is achievable with a hosted model, and it changes the platform decision rather than the application design"],a:3,
e:"These are two different projects. Self-hosted weights is not on offer for these models, so if that is genuinely the requirement the scope must change. A **data-path and residency boundary** is achievable: managed platforms let traffic and identity stay inside an existing cloud account with its own IAM, private networking and region selection. Distinguishing them early prevents months of work against an impossible constraint, and it correctly locates the decision at the **deployment platform** rather than in the application.",
w:"A — the vendor matters once the requirement is understood, but it cannot resolve which of the two requirements was meant. B — pricing is a consequence of the residency choice, not the question that establishes feasibility. C — proxy behaviour is a delivery detail, and treating it as the substance of a data-residency requirement trivialises it."},

{id:1360,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A compliance stakeholder requires that the assistant \"explain its reasoning\" for every decision. What must be clarified before this can be designed?",
o:["A. Whether the explanation is shown to end users or only to auditors","B. Whether the explanation must fit within a length limit","C. Whether a model-written rationale is acceptable, or whether the decision itself must be produced by auditable deterministic logic - because generated prose is text about the answer, not a faithful trace of how it was computed","D. Which model tier produces the most convincing explanations"],a:2,
e:"A generated explanation is **another generation**, not a log of the computation, and it can be fluent and wrong about its own basis. If the auditor must be able to re-derive a decision, the decision has to come from code: the model extracts and proposes, deterministic rules decide, and the record contains the inputs, the rule that fired and the citations. If a human-readable narrative alongside a code-made decision is enough, that is a much cheaper design. The transferable principle: **the model proposes, deterministic code authorises - and only the latter is auditable.**",
w:"A — the audience affects presentation and retention, not whether the explanation can be trusted as evidence. B — length is a formatting constraint and does not touch the faithfulness problem. D — persuasiveness is precisely the wrong criterion; a more convincing rationale for an unauditable decision is worse, not better."},

{id:1361,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A sponsor's only cost requirement is \"keep it under 4,000 pounds a month\". The team has no volume estimate. What is the right translation?",
o:["A. Choose the cheapest model tier and revisit if quality complaints arrive","B. Set a low `max_tokens` so no single request can be expensive","C. Enable prompt caching, which is the dominant cost factor in every workload","D. Derive cost per completed task from measured input and output tokens including retries and tool-loop turns, multiply by forecast volume, and state what should happen when the budget is exceeded - degrade, queue or reject"],a:3,
e:"A budget is only a requirement once it is joined to **cost per completed task** and **volume**. Measure both halves of the token bill (output is priced above input), include the multipliers people forget - retries, agent loop turns, re-sent history, cache writes - and then decide the **overflow policy**, because that is an architectural commitment. It also has an error-handling consequence: a configured organisation or workspace spend limit surfaces as a **400**, while hitting a usage-tier monthly cap surfaces as a **429**, so \"out of budget\" is a code path, not just a report.",
w:"A — picking a tier without a volume estimate cannot establish whether the budget holds, and it trades quality before any measurement exists. B — a per-request cap limits one request while the bill is driven by volume, retries and re-sent history. C — caching is a strong lever for stable prefixes and no lever at all for short, highly variable prompts; asserting it dominates every workload is wrong."},

{id:1362,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Two requirements are given: \"the assistant must always give the customer an answer\" and \"the assistant must never give tax advice\". A customer asks a tax question. What should the engineer do?",
o:["A. Implement both and let the model decide case by case","B. Implement the prohibition and return an empty response when it applies","C. Have the stakeholder state which requirement takes precedence and what the assistant should do instead - a scripted hand-off - then encode that as a test case with deterministic enforcement rather than as a prompt request","D. Split the product into two assistants and route between them"],a:2,
e:"The two requirements are **jointly unsatisfiable** on a real class of inputs, and that is a product decision rather than an engineering one. What must be elicited is the precedence rule and the **defined alternative behaviour** - the exact hand-off, referral or refusal wording - because that is what makes the requirement testable and what the eval suite asserts. Enforcement then belongs in code (topic classification, a blocked-topic gate) rather than resting on an advisory instruction.",
w:"A — leaving the conflict to per-request model judgement produces inconsistent behaviour and no defensible record of the policy. B — an empty response satisfies neither requirement and is a worse customer experience than a scripted hand-off. D — two assistants merely relocate the same conflict into the router, which still needs the precedence rule."},

{id:1363,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A specification requires generated copy to be \"professional and on brand\". What is the minimum needed to turn that into an acceptance criterion?",
o:["A. A demonstration to the stakeholder using three hand-picked examples","B. A written rubric, a fixed representative sample of inputs, a named reviewer and an agreed pass threshold, so the same set can be re-scored after any prompt or model change","C. A unit test asserting the response contains none of a list of banned words","D. A sign-off email recording that the stakeholder saw the feature"],a:1,
e:"An unfalsifiable requirement cannot be accepted or regression-tested. Making it measurable needs four parts: a **rubric** that turns \"on brand\" into scoreable criteria, a **fixed sample** that is representative rather than cherry-picked, a **named grader** so scores are comparable, and a **threshold** that defines pass. The same artefact then serves as the eval set for every later prompt or model change, which is what converts a one-off sign-off into a durable quality gate.",
w:"A — hand-picked examples measure the demo, not the system, and cannot be re-run after a change. C — a banned-word check is a useful guardrail but says nothing about tone, structure or brand fit. D — attendance at a demonstration is not a criterion; nothing about it can be re-evaluated later."},

{id:1364,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A nightly market-commentary job carries four requirements: 60,000 documents per night; results available by 07:00; cost sensitivity; and every figure quoted must be no more than 60 seconds old at the moment of generation. Which requirement most shapes the architecture?",
o:["A. The 60-second data-freshness bound, because it forbids generating ahead of time and therefore rules out the cheap overnight batch design that the other three requirements point to","B. The 60,000-document volume, because it determines the number of workers","C. The 07:00 deadline, because it sets the required drain rate","D. The cost sensitivity, because it determines the model tier"],a:0,
e:"Volume, deadline and cost together describe an ideal **Batch API** workload: a 50% discount, completion within 24 hours, up to 100,000 requests per submission, and the 1-hour cache TTL so a shared prefix survives the run. The freshness clause destroys that, because a batch's generation time is not under your control - so a figure fetched at submission could be hours stale when the text is produced. Recognising the **binding constraint** is the skill: one clause can eliminate the design the others recommend, and it should be challenged explicitly if it was written casually.",
w:"B — volume sizes the fleet within any of the candidate designs; it does not choose between them. C — a 07:00 deadline is comfortably met by an overnight batch and so discriminates between designs least. D — cost sensitivity favours the batch design that the freshness clause forbids, so it cannot be the deciding requirement."},

{id:1365,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A client asks that the assistant \"remember each user's preferences between sessions\". Which clarification most affects the design?",
o:["A. What specifically must persist, for how long, who may see it, and whether the user can inspect and delete it - because memory here is application state you store and re-inject, so retention and access rules drive the design","B. Whether the model can be fine-tuned on each user's history","C. Whether the context window is large enough to hold every past session","D. Whether the conversation store should be relational or document-oriented"],a:0,
e:"Because the API is **stateless**, \"memory\" is a store you build and a prefix you re-inject - so the requirement is really a **data** requirement. The consequential questions are the data-governance ones: which fields are retained, for how long, who can read them, whether the user can see and erase them, and whether remembered content may be quoted back verbatim. Answering those determines the schema, the retention job, the access controls and the UI, and it usually reveals that the client wants far less remembered than \"remember everything\" implies.",
w:"B — this is a storage-and-retrieval requirement, not a weight-modification one, and per-user model variants would be an extraordinary answer to it. C — persistence is not achieved by holding every session in context; that is unbounded growth, not memory. D — the storage technology is a downstream choice that follows the retention and access answers."},

{id:1366,d:"D1",s:"Understanding Requirements",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A stakeholder's written requirement reads: \"Use retrieval-augmented generation with a vector database.\" What should the engineer do first?",
o:["A. Build it as specified, since the requirement is unambiguous","B. Benchmark three vector databases and recommend one","C. Elicit the underlying need and its acceptance criteria - corpus size, update frequency, citation and accuracy expectations - because a stated solution is not a requirement, and a small stable corpus may be better served by a cached prefix","D. Ask which embedding model should be used"],a:2,
e:"A solution presented as a requirement hides the constraints that would let you evaluate it. The questions that decide the design are corpus **size** (does it fit in a request you can afford?), **volatility** (how fresh must answers be?), whether answers must **cite** sources, and what accuracy is acceptable. For a corpus of a few thousand tokens that changes quarterly, a cached prefix is simpler, cheaper and has no recall failure mode. Principle: **capture the need and the acceptance criteria; let the architecture be a conclusion, not an input.**",
w:"A — the statement is clear as an instruction and silent as a requirement: it says nothing about freshness, citation or accuracy. B — tool selection is premature before the need is established, and may be moot. D — an embedding-model choice presupposes the retrieval design is correct."},

{id:1367,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A graded eval suite of 900 live cases takes 40 minutes and costs real money per run. How should it be positioned in CI?",
o:["A. Run the full suite on every pull request and accept the cost, because quality is the priority","B. Run it only immediately before each release","C. Replace the live suite with mocked assertions against expected strings so it can run on every commit","D. Tier the gates: deterministic mocked tests on every pull request, a small stratified live smoke eval on pull requests that touch prompts, tools or model configuration, and the full graded suite nightly and before release"],a:3,
e:"Eval suites and unit tests answer different questions and need different cadences. **Mocked unit tests** are fast, free and deterministic, so they gate every change. A **stratified smoke eval** of a few dozen cases catches gross regressions on the changes that can actually cause them - prompt, tool and model-configuration edits - within a tolerable PR wait. The **full graded suite** runs nightly and as a release gate, where 40 minutes is affordable and the score threshold has teeth. Principle: **gate proportionally to blast radius and cost.**",
w:"A — a 40-minute paid gate on every pull request makes engineers batch changes and route around CI, which reduces quality. B — a release-time gate cannot attribute a regression to a change, so someone must bisect several days of work. C — exact-string assertions on generated text are flaky and cannot measure quality, which is the whole purpose of the suite."},

{id:1368,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A team builds its golden dataset by recording the current system's outputs and labelling them \"expected\". Why does this eval fail to protect them?",
o:["A. Because recorded outputs cannot be stored in version control","B. Because the expected answers encode today's behaviour rather than the correct answer, so every improvement scores as a regression and every existing error is frozen in as correct","C. Because an eval set must contain at least 1,000 cases to be statistically meaningful","D. Because generated outputs cannot be compared automatically"],a:1,
e:"A golden set must be labelled from the **requirement**, not from the system under test - otherwise it measures self-similarity. Two failures follow: genuine improvements are penalised because they differ from the recording, and every bug present at capture time becomes an assertion that the bug must persist. Build the set from the specification or from expert judgement, deliberately include known failures, edge cases and adversarial inputs, and keep provenance notes so a disputed label can be re-examined.",
w:"A — recorded outputs are perfectly storable in version control; that is where a de-identified eval set belongs. C — set size affects statistical power, but a small well-labelled set beats a large self-referential one. D — automated comparison is entirely feasible with rubric graders, extraction assertions or schema checks; the problem is what is being compared against."},

{id:1369,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"After 30 rounds of prompt tuning against the same 120-case eval, the score has risen from 71% to 94% while production complaint rates are unchanged. What is happening and what is the fix?",
o:["A. The eval set is too small ever to reach 94%, so the score is an artefact","B. Repeated tuning against one set overfits it: keep a held-out test set that is scored rarely and reported as the headline number, and refresh cases from production traffic","C. The grader has become too lenient, so the threshold should be raised","D. The underlying model changed during the work, so it needs re-pinning"],a:1,
e:"An eval you optimise against stops being an unbiased estimate of unseen performance - after 30 rounds the prompt has been fitted to those 120 cases, including their idiosyncrasies. The standard remedy is a **split**: a train set you iterate on freely, a validation set for round-by-round decisions, and a **held-out test set** scored infrequently and treated as the real number. Refresh cases from live traffic so the distribution keeps up with how users actually behave.",
w:"A — set size limits precision, not the attainable score; 94% on 120 cases is entirely possible and still misleading here. C — nothing has changed about the grader; the prompt has been fitted to the cases it grades. D — a changed model would move the score without 30 rounds of deliberate tuning, and pinning does not address overfitting."},

{id:1370,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A canary carries 5% of traffic with a new prompt. Error rate, latency and 5xx counts are identical across arms, so the change is rolled out fully. A week later complaints arrive about answers that stop mid-list. Which comparison would have caught it?",
o:["A. Average latency per arm","B. The 429 rate per arm","C. Cache hit rate per arm","D. The `stop_reason` distribution per arm - truncation returns a successful response carrying `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`, so it is invisible to error-rate monitoring"],a:3,
e:"A truncated answer is an **HTTP 200 with a complete, well-formed body**; the only in-band signal is `stop_reason`. Tracking the **distribution** of `stop_reason` values per arm - `end_turn` versus `max_tokens`, `tool_use`, `refusal`, `pause_turn` - turns silent quality loss into a visible metric shift, and it costs nothing because the field is already on every response. Remember the enum is documented as extensible, so the dashboard needs a bucket for values it does not recognise.",
w:"A — a longer prompt that truncates output can leave latency essentially unchanged, and latency is not what users complained about. B — throttling is unrelated to a prompt that makes responses run past the output cap. C — cache hit rate would legitimately move for any prompt edit, so it reports the change without revealing its consequence."},

{id:1371,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A service has not changed its code, prompt, parameters or pinned model id for two months, yet users report gradually worse answers. Which monitoring practice is designed to detect this?",
o:["A. Alerting on the 429 rate","B. Tracking p99 latency","C. Continuously sampling a fraction of real responses and grading them against the rubric, so the quality trend is measured rather than inferred","D. Re-running the CI unit test suite nightly"],a:2,
e:"Quality can drift with nothing in your repository changing. The usual cause is **input drift** - new customers, new document types, new phrasings - and a secondary one is that a pinned model id fixes the **weights** while serving infrastructure can change, so pinning buys stability rather than bit-identical output. Neither shows up in error rates or latency. A **sampled quality score**, graded by rubric (human or model-as-judge) and plotted as a trend, is the only signal that moves for this, and it is what closes the loop between an eval suite and production reality.",
w:"A — throttling is a capacity signal and is unrelated to answer quality. B — latency and quality are independent; responses can get worse without getting slower. D — unit tests assert code behaviour against fixed fixtures, so they pass unchanged while live quality slides."},

{id:1372,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A canary is live behind a flag. When quality drops, the team discovers that disabling the flag requires a configuration deploy taking 25 minutes, and that part of the change was baked into the container image. What should have been done?",
o:["A. Exercise the rollback during the canary - flip the flag off once to prove the path works inside the target time - and ensure the change is entirely flag-controlled rather than partly baked into the deployed artefact","B. Run two container images side by side and swap traffic at the load balancer instead","C. Shorten the canary window so less traffic is exposed","D. Add an automatic rollback triggered by the error rate"],a:0,
e:"A rollback plan is only real once it has been **executed**. For an LLM feature the plan must cover more than code: the prompt version, the model id, the parameter set including effort, the tool set, and any schema - all of which must be restorable together and without a deploy, or the mean time to recovery is a pipeline duration. Proving the path during the canary, while the blast radius is 5%, is cheap; discovering it during an incident is not.",
w:"B — blue-green deployment is a fine mechanism, but the defect is an unproven rollback path and a change split between flag and artefact, which recurs regardless of mechanism. C — less exposure reduces harm per incident but does nothing about a 25-minute recovery. D — the regression was a quality drop with a flat error rate, so an error-rate trigger would never have fired."},

{id:1373,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An organisation runs the same Claude model first-party for its EU service and on a partner cloud platform for its US service. The migration plan applies the first-party retirement date to both. What is wrong?",
o:["A. Retirement dates are contractually synchronised across all platforms","B. Partner platforms never retire models; they only stop accepting new subscriptions","C. Lifecycle dates on partner-operated platforms are set by the partner and can differ from the first-party schedule, so each deployment needs its own migration date taken from that platform's notices","D. The absence of `-latest` aliases means partner platforms absorb retirements automatically"],a:2,
e:"Model lifecycle is **per platform**. Deprecation and retirement dates on partner-operated platforms are set by the partner and can differ from the first-party schedule, so a single date in the plan will be wrong for at least one deployment. Track each platform's own deprecation notices, plan against the **earliest** applicable date per deployment, and remember that a migration is not just a string swap: it needs regression evaluation, a token-count re-baseline, and a re-check of cached prefixes.",
w:"A — the schedules are related but not contractually identical, which is exactly the trap here. B — models are genuinely retired on partner platforms, after which requests naming them fail. D — there are no `-latest` aliases on any platform; current ids are dateless pinned snapshots, and nothing absorbs a retirement for you."},

{id:1374,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"After a model upgrade the prompts are byte-identical, yet `input_tokens` per request has risen by roughly 30% and one short route's `cache_read_input_tokens` has fallen to zero. What is the explanation?",
o:["A. Tokenization is model-specific - a newer tokenizer can produce around 30% more tokens for the same text - and the minimum cacheable prefix length varies by model, so token counts must be re-baselined with `count_tokens` and every cached prefix re-checked against the new model's minimum","B. The upgrade enabled thinking, whose tokens are billed as input","C. `count_tokens` is inaccurate for newer models, so the increase is a reporting artefact","D. Cache read counters are not reported for newer models"],a:0,
e:"Tokenizers belong to models. A newer tokenizer can encode the same text into roughly **30% more tokens** (about 2.5 characters per token rather than about 4), so cost, context headroom and any token budget all shift on an upgrade with no prompt change at all. Separately, the **minimum cacheable prefix length varies by model**, so a prefix that qualified before may now fall short and silently stop caching. Both are why a model upgrade needs a measurement pass - re-baseline with the free `count_tokens` endpoint and verify cache reads - not just a regression eval.",
w:"B — thinking tokens count toward generated output and the context window, not toward input tokens. C — `count_tokens` returns a tokenizer-specific estimate for the model you name; the increase is real and is reflected in billing. D — cache counters are reported normally; a zero here means no read occurred."},

{id:1375,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A canary runs at 1% for 20 minutes - about 300 requests - and is declared safe. The failure mode it was meant to catch occurs in roughly 1 request in 400 of a document type that arrives mainly at month end. What was wrong with the canary?",
o:["A. 1% is always too small; it should have started at 50%","B. The canary had neither enough exposures nor a representative traffic mix: size it by expected exposures of the failure mode and cover a representative traffic cycle, and where the case is too rare, cover it with a targeted eval instead of waiting for it to occur","C. Only aggregate latency and error rate should be judged at low volume, since nothing else is reliable","D. The canary should simply have run indefinitely, since more time always resolves the question"],a:1,
e:"A canary is a **sampling experiment**, and its conclusion is only as strong as the number of exposures of the thing you care about. At 1 in 400, roughly 300 requests give you a coin-flip chance of seeing the case even once - and because the relevant document type is concentrated at month end, the window was not representative either. Size canaries by expected exposures and cover a full traffic cycle; for genuinely rare inputs, force them deterministically in an eval rather than hoping production supplies them.",
w:"A — a large initial exposure defeats the purpose of a canary, which is to limit blast radius; the defect is the exposure count for the specific case. C — restricting attention to aggregate metrics guarantees you miss the quality signals that matter, such as the `stop_reason` distribution. D — an indefinite canary is not a rollout plan, and it still cannot manufacture a case that only arrives at month end."},

{id:1376,d:"D1",s:"Systems Life Cycle",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An eval suite scores single-turn answers and passes at 96%. After release the agent calls the wrong tool, and in some sessions never terminates. What must the suite include?",
o:["A. A larger single-turn set of about 2,000 cases","B. A wall-clock timeout on the loop, which is sufficient protection","C. Stricter grading of the final answer text","D. Multi-turn trajectories against mocked tools, asserting the tool call sequence, the terminating `stop_reason`, turn and token budgets, and behaviour when a tool returns an error"],a:3,
e:"An agent's behaviour lives in the **loop**, not in a single response, so a single-turn eval cannot see tool selection, recovery or termination. A trajectory eval fixes tool behaviour with mocks and asserts on the path: which tools were called and in what order, that the run ended on `end_turn` rather than exhausting a budget, that turn and token ceilings held, and that an `is_error` tool result produced sane recovery rather than a retry storm. Principle: **evaluate the unit that can fail - here, the trajectory.**",
w:"A — more single-turn cases sample the same blind spot more thoroughly. B — a timeout truncates a runaway loop but detects nothing before release and does not address wrong tool selection. C — a stricter grader on final prose still never observes the tool sequence or the termination condition."},

{id:1377,d:"D1",s:"Deployment Platforms",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A working first-party integration is ported to Claude on Google Cloud. Which request-shape differences must the code account for?",
o:["A. `model` moves into an `anthropic-model` header while the API version remains a header","B. `messages` must be renamed and roles must be re-labelled","C. The body is unchanged; only the credential header differs","D. `model` is not sent in the body - it forms part of the endpoint URL - and `anthropic_version` is sent in the body, set to the platform's version value, rather than as a header"],a:3,
e:"On Google Cloud the **transport envelope** differs while the payload semantics do not: the model identifier is part of the endpoint path, and `anthropic_version` moves from a header into the request body with a platform-specific value. Everything that carries the intelligence carries over untouched - `messages`, content blocks, `system`, tool definitions, `tool_result` pairing, `stop_reason`, `usage`, `cache_control`. Credentials become Google Cloud application default credentials rather than an Anthropic key, and project plus region become required configuration. Principle: **prompts and message semantics port; envelopes, credentials and feature availability do not.**",
w:"A — there is no model header; the model appears in the URL, and the version moves into the body. B — message and role semantics are unchanged, which is what makes prompts portable. C — the body is not unchanged: the model is absent from it and `anthropic_version` is added to it."},

{id:1378,d:"D1",s:"Deployment Platforms",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"On Amazon Bedrock, a request using a model's base identifier returns a 400 stating that invocation with on-demand throughput is not supported and to retry with an inference profile. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Request model access for the account in the console, since the identifier is valid","B. Pass an inference-profile identifier - the base id carrying a routing prefix, or the full inference-profile ARN - because the model is offered through cross-region inference rather than on-demand throughput","C. Switch to the specific region in which the model is physically hosted","D. Append a date suffix to the model id to select a snapshot"],a:1,
e:"Newer models on Bedrock are served through **cross-region inference**, so the invocable identifier is an inference profile, not the base model id: the base id with a routing prefix (a global prefix for dynamic routing, or a regional one such as a US or EU prefix), or the profile's full ARN. Regional routing exists for data residency and carries a pricing premium relative to global routing. This is the clearest example of a change that does not port: **the model-identifier format is platform-specific even though the request body is not.**",
w:"A — a missing model-access grant produces an access error, not this specific throughput message. C — the routing prefix, not the client region, is what selects an inference profile; changing region alone leaves the same rejection. D — first-party ids are dateless pinned snapshots and must never be date-suffixed, and inventing a suffix does not create an inference profile."},

{id:1379,d:"D1",s:"Deployment Platforms",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team standardised on a partner cloud platform for compliance reasons. Their nightly re-classification job was designed around the Message Batches endpoint's discount and 24-hour window. What must change?",
o:["A. Message Batches is not available on that platform; re-plan the job as rate-limited asynchronous Messages API traffic, or keep that one workload on the first-party API if the compliance boundary permits","B. Batches are available, but the discount does not apply on partner platforms","C. Batches are available, but a submission is capped at 1,000 requests there","D. Submit the batch first-party and retrieve the results through the partner platform's endpoint"],a:0,
e:"The Message Batches endpoint is a **first-party API surface** and is not offered on Amazon Bedrock or Google Cloud, so a design built on its economics does not port. The realistic options are to run the work as throttled asynchronous traffic against the Messages API within the account's rate limits, or to keep that single workload first-party if the residency rules allow a carve-out. The general lesson: **check the platform's own feature-support page before committing a design to it** - the Files API, URL-based image and document sources, the Models endpoint and several server-side tools are also commonly absent.",
w:"B — the endpoint itself is absent, so there is no discount to apply or withhold. C — there is no reduced per-submission cap on those platforms because the endpoint is not offered; first-party limits are 100,000 requests or 256 MB, whichever comes first. D — batches are not a cross-platform service; results are retrieved from the same API that accepted the submission."},

{id:1380,d:"D1",s:"Deployment Platforms",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A document pipeline that works first-party begins failing on a partner cloud platform for large scanned files, well below the model's token limit. The requests carry several base64-encoded page images plus a long conversation history. What is the most likely cause?",
o:["A. The context window is smaller on partner platforms, so the token limit was reached first","B. `max_tokens` must be lowered to reduce the request size","C. The platform enforces its own HTTP request payload ceiling measured in megabytes; base64 encoding inflates the body, and with no Files API the bytes must be resent every turn - so downscale the images, split the document across requests, or keep the workload where a `file_id` reference is available","D. Adding a cache breakpoint will stop the images being re-sent"],a:2,
e:"Partner platforms impose their **own payload size ceilings in megabytes**, independent of the token limit, and base64 expands binary data by about a third. Because the **Files API and URL image sources are unavailable there**, image bytes must be resent on every turn, so a long multimodal conversation grows the body relentlessly. Mitigations are byte-level: downscale pages (which also cuts visual tokens, counted in 28x28 patches), split the document, or keep that workload on a surface where a `file_id` reference replaces the bytes. The first-party analogue of this failure is **413 `request_too_large`**.",
w:"A — current models carry the same large context window on those platforms, and the symptom appears well below the token limit. B — `max_tokens` caps generated output and has no effect on the size of the request you upload. D — caching affects billing and processing of a matched prefix; the full request body is still transmitted on every call."},

{id:1381,d:"D1",s:"Deployment Platforms",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A design depends on the server-side web search tool. What should the team confirm before choosing a deployment platform?",
o:["A. That server-side tool availability differs by platform - web search is offered on some managed platforms and not others, and where it is offered the available tool version may be an older variant - so confirm it on the target platform's feature-support page and keep a client-side fetch path as the alternative","B. Nothing: server-side tools are part of the Messages API and are therefore available wherever it is","C. That server-side tools are declared through `mcp_servers` rather than `tools` on partner platforms","D. That the Files API is enabled, since server-side tools depend on it"],a:0,
e:"**Feature availability is the main axis on which deployment platforms differ.** Message and content-block semantics, prompts, tool definitions and `stop_reason` port cleanly; the surrounding surface does not - server-side tools such as web search, web fetch and code execution, the Files API, URL input sources, the Models endpoint and Message Batches all vary, and even where a tool exists the offered version can lag. Design defensively: put the capability behind your own interface and keep an in-house fetch-and-pass path so the platform choice stays reversible.",
w:"B — being part of the Messages API does not imply universal availability; several tools are explicitly unavailable on some platforms. C — `mcp_servers` is a different, separately gated feature; server-side tools are declared in `tools` wherever they are offered. D — the Files API is an unrelated surface, and its own availability differs by platform."},

{id:1382,d:"D1",s:"Deployment Platforms",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which credential authenticates a request to Claude on Amazon Bedrock?",
o:["A. An Anthropic API key sent as `x-api-key`","B. AWS credentials with SigV4 request signing, handled by the platform client - or a Bedrock-issued bearer token - together with a configured region","C. An Anthropic API key sent as `Authorization: Bearer`, plus an AWS region","D. An Anthropic API key registered against the AWS account in the console"],a:1,
e:"On Bedrock, identity comes from **AWS**: the platform SDK client resolves AWS credentials (a role, environment variables or a profile) and performs **SigV4 request signing**, with a Bedrock-issued bearer token available as an alternative for environments that cannot manage AWS credentials. A **region** must be resolvable. An Anthropic API key has no meaning there. That is exactly the appeal for an enterprise: the integration inherits existing IAM, private networking and consolidated cloud billing - while the message payload it sends stays the same.",
w:"A — `x-api-key` is a valid first-party credential header and is not how Bedrock authenticates. C — `Authorization: Bearer` with an Anthropic key is also a first-party form; adding a region does not make it a Bedrock credential. D — Anthropic keys are not registrable AWS credentials; there is no such console association."}
);
